<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Moskva River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Moskva River is a river of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, about 470 kilometres long, which rises on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> Upland and flows east through Moscow to join the Oka at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolomna">Kolomna</a>. The city of Moscow grew on its banks from a small settlement first mentioned in 1147, and control of the river and its route towards the Oka and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> helped the early princes of Moscow extend their power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moskva-river.jpg" alt="Moskva River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Basile Starynkevitch · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moskva_kremlin_04_2007.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
